  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Persistence / Account Manipulation — Additional Cloud Roles", "technique": "T1098.003", "what_happened": "At 2026-05-13 08:02:11 CEST, Wazuh's Azure module logged an Azure AD directory audit event. A standard, non-privileged user account (actor 'alice.jones') first issued a read against the Global Administrator role membership, then issued an 'Add member to role' operation that placed the attacker-controlled principal a1b2c3d4-e5f6-7890-abcd-ef1234567890 into the Global Administrator role (role template 62e90394-69f5-4237-9190-012177145e10). The same actor immediately attempted an Owner role assignment at subscription scope for the same principal. Wazuh fired custom rule 92320 ('Azure: Add member to Global Administrator role') and 92322 ('Priv grant from non-PAW workstation'). The actor 'alice.jones' has no record of any IAM mutations in the prior 90 days and the initiating session did not originate from a Privileged Access Workstation — a high-fidelity T1098.003 privilege-grant signature.", "chain_reason": "rule.groups contained 'azure' and rule.description matched 'Add member to role' / 'Global Administrator' naming the actor alice.jones and the target principal a1b2c3d4-e5f6-7890-abcd-ef1234567890, with rule.mitre.id T1098.003. The univariate signature: a non-privileged actor with zero prior IAM mutations performing a Global Administrator role-membership add plus an Owner subscription assignment within a 4-minute window.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T08:02:11+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Persistence / Account Manipulation — Additional Cloud Roles", "technique": "T1098.003", "what_happened": "At 08:08:40 CEST (~6 minutes after the Azure role add), Wazuh's AWS CloudTrail module logged an IAM enumeration burst (iam:ListUsers) followed by an AttachUserPolicy operation that bound the AWS managed policy arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AdministratorAccess to the IAM user 'svc-bot'. A CreateAccessKey call against the same 'svc-bot' user followed, minting a fresh long-lived programmatic credential. Wazuh fired custom rule 92321 ('AWS: AdministratorAccess policy attached to user'). This is the cross-cloud continuation of the privilege-grant chain: having taken Global Administrator in Azure AD, the same operator pivoted into the AWS tenant and granted full administrative IAM rights plus a passwordless access key to a service account.", "chain_reason": "rule.groups contained 'aws' and rule.description matched 'AttachUserPolicy' / 'AdministratorAccess' naming the IAM user svc-bot, with rule.mitre.id T1098.003 and rule.id 92321. The AttachUserPolicy(AdministratorAccess) followed by CreateAccessKey on the same non-admin service account is the canonical AWS persistence-via-additional-cloud-roles signature, and it lands in the same incident window as the Azure Global Administrator add.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T08:08:40+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Persistence / Account Manipulation — Additional Local or Domain Groups", "technique": "T1098.003", "what_happened": "From 08:11:30 to 08:14:52 CEST, the on-prem Active Directory continuation fired. An LDAP query enumerated the Domain Admins group membership on the domain controller 10.108.57.10, immediately followed by a Security event 4728 ('A member was added to a security-enabled global group') that placed the account 'svc.bot' into the Domain Admins group. Wazuh fired the EID 4728 detection (rule 4728) plus the correlation rule for privilege grant from a non-PAW workstation. The attacker has now established a third independent persistence path — full domain dominance on-prem — completing a cross-platform Azure + AWS + on-prem AD privilege-grant chain that survives any password reset of the originally compromised 'alice.jones' account.", "chain_reason": "rule.id 4728 fired with rule.description matching 'member was added' / 'Domain Admins' naming the member svc.bot and the agent being the domain controller 10.108.57.10, with rule.mitre.id T1098.003. EID 4728 against the Domain Admins group, preceded by an LDAP enumeration of that same group from a non-PAW source and landing inside the same incident window as the Azure and AWS grants, is the on-prem leg of the same operator's cross-platform privilege-grant campaign.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T08:11:30+0200"}
